{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 14, :comments ["<b>Shamed seven days.</b> Rashi explained: a fortiori, for the Shekhinah — fourteen days. And the world asks: where is this measure of fourteen days found? And one may say that according to strict law it is in tractate Niddah, chapter haMappelet. Our Rabbis taught: there are three partners in a person — the Holy One, blessed be He, his father, and his mother. His father sows the white substance, from which come sinews, bones, nails, the brain in the head, and the white of the eyes — here are five from the father. And his mother sows the red substance, from which come blood, flesh, etc. The Holy One, blessed be He, plants in him spirit, soul, facial appearance, knowledge, understanding, intelligence, sight of the eyes, hearing of the ears, speech of the lips, and walking of the feet. Here are ten things from the Holy One, blessed be He — double those of his father. And therefore in the reproof here He took fourteen, which is double the seven of her father — except that the claimant according to strict law should only be as great as the case at hand, and she should not be shamed more than seven. And this is difficult to Rabbenu Bekhor Shor: do we say in every stringent case derived from a lenient one that we add according to how much more stringent it is than it? And if you say a fortiori, for the Shekhinah fourteen — that is forever, for at the end of the seclusion, which is fourteen days, if the metzora has not healed, he declares it permanent. And Rabbi Baruch bar Yitzchak said that in the two seclusions there are no more than thirteen days, for the seventh day counts both here and there. Nevertheless, the question remains: from where do we learn that the reproof of her father is 'shamed seven days'? For according to the reading of the verse it seems straightforward from another place that from her father she is shamed seven days. And one may say that the reproof of her father is in the case of one banished, and one banished is like a mourner, who observes seven. And it is still difficult, for the seven of mourning we do not find in Scripture — it is only rabbinic. And furthermore, what does it mean that 'her father spit in her face,' when he was not alive at that time? However, it seems to me that from this there is no difficulty at all — for this is what it means to say: if he were alive and spat in her face. Nevertheless, there are those who explain that her father reproved her in Egypt, at the birth of Moses — that her father slapped her on the head when he was cast into the Nile, and said to her, 'My daughter, where is your prophecy? Will you not be shamed seven days?' For there it is written: ״ותתצב אחותו מרחוק״ — just as with a metzora, seven, so too here, seven. However, I do not know where 'far off' is written regarding a metzora, unless it is because it is written ״וישב מחוץ לאהלו שבעת ימים״, which is language of distancing. And from this verse, ״תכלם שבעת ימים״, it is proven in tractate Moed Katan, chapter veElu Megalchin, that rabbinic reproof is seven days."], :book :numbers, :voice :daat-zkenim, :lang :en, :chapter 12, :notes {0 "The word ריהטא appears to be a variant or error for קריאתא/קרא (reading of the verse); rendered literally as 'reading.' The phrase דיו לבא מן הדין להיות כנדון is a standard talmudic legal principle (the claimant from strict law should only be as great as the case at hand)."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:09:33.281460937Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}